course: dictionary
lesson: 11256
title: Wound: hurt, or wind
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: One word can have more than one meaning, and the words around it show you which one. Wound can be said two ways. Said «woond», it means an injury or to hurt someone. Said «wownd», it is the past of wind.
explain: «She cleaned the wound and covered it with a plaster.» Cleaned and plaster are clues that a wound is a cut or damage on the body. «His cruel words wounded her.» Here wounded means hurt her feelings, not her skin, because words cannot cut skin.
explain: «I wound the string around the kite stick.» Wound said «wownd» means you wound it in the past, like yesterday. To check, swap in a meaning and read the sentence again. Does «I hurt the string around the kite stick» make sense? It does not, so this wound must be the past of wind.
fact: A wound is an injury where the skin or body is cut, broken or damaged.
fact: To wound someone means to hurt them, and it can also mean to hurt their feelings.
fact: Wound said «wownd», rhyming with round, is the past of wind: yesterday you wound the clock.
fact: «A deep wound» means a serious cut, and «wounded pride» means hurt feelings.
fact: «Wound up» can mean wound tightly, or be very nervous or annoyed.
exercise: «The soldier's wound was cleaned and bandaged.» What is a wound? | A loud noise in the night | *An injury where the body is cut or damaged | A kind of wool used for knitting | A small wooden toy
exercise: «His unkind joke wounded her feelings.» What does wounded mean here? | *Hurt her feelings | Made her laugh loudly | Wrapped her in string | Covered her with a bandage
exercise: «I wound the string around the kite stick.» How is wound said in this sentence? | Wownd, and it means an injury | Woond, and it means to wind a clock | *Wownd, rhyming with round | Woond, rhyming with moon
test: «The clock stopped, so Mum wound it up again.» What does wound mean here? | A loud bang | *Wound up, the past of wind | To hurt someone | A cut on the skin
test: «The knight was wounded in the battle, but he fought on.» What does wounded mean here? | *Injured, with a cut or damage to the body | Rested, in a warm bed | Made of wood | Fed by the king
exam: Which sentence uses wound to mean hurt someone's feelings? | «The clock was wound each night.» | «The wound was cleaned with warm water.» | «She wound the kite string tightly.» | *«Her cruel words wounded him deeply.»
